WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:04.171
- And I give it my all,
I practice every day,

00:00:04.171 --> 00:00:07.764
and I try and stay authentic
and uncompromising.

00:00:07.764 --> 00:00:12.238
I could probably rob banks
or burglarize jewelry stores

00:00:12.238 --> 00:00:13.875
and be very clever about it.

00:00:13.875 --> 00:00:16.958
(clarinet squeeling)

00:00:21.178 --> 00:00:23.158
- Hello, Believe Nation,
my name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:23.158 --> 00:00:25.939
My one word is believe, and
I believe that entrepreneurs

00:00:25.939 --> 00:00:28.748
will solve all of the
world's major problems.

00:00:28.748 --> 00:00:31.056
So today we're going to learn
from legendary director,

00:00:31.056 --> 00:00:33.038
actor, and comedian, Woody Allen,

00:00:33.038 --> 00:00:36.077
and my take on his top
10 rules of success.

00:00:36.077 --> 00:00:37.916
Rule number two is my personal favorite,

00:00:37.916 --> 00:00:40.454
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:40.454 --> 00:00:42.032
And as always, as you're watching,

00:00:42.032 --> 00:00:44.128
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:44.128 --> 00:00:45.822
please leave it in the comments below.

00:00:45.822 --> 00:00:48.175
Put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired.

00:00:48.175 --> 00:00:49.839
You might win a prize as well.

00:00:49.839 --> 00:00:51.303
And also, when you write it down,

00:00:51.303 --> 00:00:54.148
it's much more likely to
stick for yourself as well.

00:00:54.148 --> 00:00:54.981
Enjoy.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:01.207
(inspirational music)

00:01:08.073 --> 00:01:12.566
- The business has changed,
filmmaking is the same.

00:01:12.566 --> 00:01:14.906
I do the same thing now as I
did on Take the Money and Run.

00:01:14.906 --> 00:01:17.156
I go in in the morning, and

00:01:18.874 --> 00:01:20.874
instinctively, actually,

00:01:21.938 --> 00:01:24.242
you have a vision 'cause
you wrote the script,

00:01:24.242 --> 00:01:26.530
so you know what you want on the screen,

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:29.601
and you execute to try and get as close

00:01:29.601 --> 00:01:32.026
to that vision as possible.

00:01:32.026 --> 00:01:34.803
You never really come that close to it,

00:01:34.803 --> 00:01:38.636
but you die trying to
get as close as you can.

00:01:39.854 --> 00:01:44.737
The fun is in writing the
film and then casting it,

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:48.856
and getting the costumes, and working with

00:01:48.856 --> 00:01:52.748
talented people, and
sophisticated, gifted men,

00:01:52.748 --> 00:01:56.997
and charming, beautiful women,
and Cole Porter's music.

00:01:56.997 --> 00:01:59.482
And you work for a year on something,

00:01:59.482 --> 00:02:02.065
or six months on
something, and it's great,

00:02:02.065 --> 00:02:06.043
and it's fun, and you're
solving solvable problems.

00:02:06.043 --> 00:02:09.504
If I can't get my third
act on the film right,

00:02:09.504 --> 00:02:11.337
it doesn't mean I die.

00:02:12.409 --> 00:02:15.077
It just means that the film won't be good,

00:02:15.077 --> 00:02:18.324
and maybe I can solve that
problem, and I think about it,

00:02:18.324 --> 00:02:21.168
and it's a fun way to spend your life.

00:02:21.168 --> 00:02:22.573
And then it's over.

00:02:22.573 --> 00:02:25.116
If you're thinking of
rewards, if you're thinking

00:02:25.116 --> 00:02:28.411
of box office, or that
critics have to like it,

00:02:28.411 --> 00:02:30.883
or that the public has to like it,

00:02:30.883 --> 00:02:34.216
you're dead, you're depending on things,

00:02:35.181 --> 00:02:37.312
you're getting your
kicks in the wrong area.

00:02:37.312 --> 00:02:41.199
Your kicks have to be making the film,

00:02:41.199 --> 00:02:44.481
the actual joy of making the film,

00:02:44.481 --> 00:02:48.748
and the disappointments, and
how you circumnavigate them

00:02:48.748 --> 00:02:51.575
and solve the problems and all that.

00:02:51.575 --> 00:02:54.018
And then when it's over, you
look at it and you think,

00:02:54.018 --> 00:02:56.896
"Oh, God, when I wrote it it was so great,

00:02:56.896 --> 00:02:58.355
"and now it's so terrible.

00:02:58.355 --> 00:03:01.608
"How did this happen, what went wrong?

00:03:01.608 --> 00:03:03.901
"I made all the wrong choices."

00:03:03.901 --> 00:03:06.555
And you figure, "Well, the
next one will be better."

00:03:06.555 --> 00:03:09.254
And you put it aside and
you go on to the next film.

00:03:09.254 --> 00:03:10.553
But if you're there and you're thinking,

00:03:10.553 --> 00:03:12.898
"Well, I can't wait till
the critics see this,

00:03:12.898 --> 00:03:16.331
"I can't wait till the
public breaks the doors down,

00:03:16.331 --> 00:03:18.653
"and the box office records are ..."

00:03:18.653 --> 00:03:22.346
then you're dependent for your enjoyment

00:03:22.346 --> 00:03:26.141
on other people's taste and
other people's opinions.

00:03:26.141 --> 00:03:29.099
And I'm not interested in their opinion.

00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:30.667
The first film I ever worked on,

00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:34.346
What's New Pussycat, was a loathsome film.

00:03:34.346 --> 00:03:36.263
I hated the experience.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.364
I was going to the script writer.

00:03:38.364 --> 00:03:40.280
The took my script and mangled it.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.686
They put the film out,
the film was a big hit.

00:03:42.686 --> 00:03:45.519
It made the most money of any comedy

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:48.113
to its time, at that time.

00:03:48.113 --> 00:03:50.094
And I got no joy out of it.

00:03:50.094 --> 00:03:52.806
I was embarrassed by it, I hated it.

00:03:52.806 --> 00:03:57.256
So you don't get joy
from critical success or

00:03:57.256 --> 00:03:59.506
public success, you get joy

00:04:00.417 --> 00:04:01.584
from realizing

00:04:02.791 --> 00:04:05.632
the work of art that
you were trying to make

00:04:05.632 --> 00:04:09.661
when you were laying on
your bed writing the script.

00:04:09.661 --> 00:04:12.410
And then, all of a
sudden, you visualize it

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:14.784
and you've made it happen.

00:04:14.784 --> 00:04:18.657
You've spent $15 million or something,

00:04:18.657 --> 00:04:21.824
or more sometimes, and made it happen.

00:04:22.743 --> 00:04:26.160
And you say, "Gee, I'm proud of this."

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.889
And I remember very clearly,
I would walk the streets,

00:04:28.889 --> 00:04:29.888
I walked and I walked.

00:04:29.888 --> 00:04:31.933
I didn't know what was
going through my mind.

00:04:31.933 --> 00:04:35.869
It all seemed so violent and unreal to me.

00:04:35.869 --> 00:04:39.521
And I wandered for a long
time on the Upper West Side.

00:04:39.521 --> 00:04:40.728
It must have been hours.

00:04:40.728 --> 00:04:43.329
My feet hurt and my head was pounding.

00:04:43.329 --> 00:04:45.796
And I had to sit down, I
went into a movie house.

00:04:45.796 --> 00:04:47.160
I didn't know what was
playing or anything.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:51.122
I just needed a moment to gather
my thoughts and be logical

00:04:51.122 --> 00:04:54.782
and put the world back
into rational perspective.

00:04:54.782 --> 00:04:57.615
(helmets ringing)

00:05:03.405 --> 00:05:06.662
And I went upstairs to the
balcony, and I sat down.

00:05:06.662 --> 00:05:10.148
And the movie was a film
that I'd seen many times

00:05:10.148 --> 00:05:14.043
in my life since I was a
kid, and I always loved it.

00:05:14.043 --> 00:05:16.525
And, you know, I'm watching
these people up on the screen,

00:05:16.525 --> 00:05:19.688
and I started getting hooked
on the film, you know.

00:05:19.688 --> 00:05:23.016
And I started to feel,
"How can you even think

00:05:23.016 --> 00:05:25.823
"of killing yourself, I
mean, isn't it so stupid?

00:05:25.823 --> 00:05:28.008
"I mean, look at all the
people up there on the screen.

00:05:28.008 --> 00:05:29.976
"They're real funny.

00:05:29.976 --> 00:05:31.624
"And what if the worst is true?

00:05:31.624 --> 00:05:33.981
"What if there's no God
and you only go around

00:05:33.981 --> 00:05:35.541
"once and that's it?

00:05:35.541 --> 00:05:38.376
"Well, you know, don't you want
to be part of the experience?

00:05:38.376 --> 00:05:41.081
"You know, what the hell,
it's not all a drag."

00:05:41.081 --> 00:05:43.546
And I'm thinking to myself,
"Geez, I should stop

00:05:43.546 --> 00:05:47.182
"ruining my life searching for
answers I'm never going to get,

00:05:47.182 --> 00:05:49.688
"and just enjoy it while it lasts.

00:05:49.688 --> 00:05:52.255
"And, you know, after, who knows?

00:05:52.255 --> 00:05:53.172
"I mean, you know, maybe
there is something.

00:05:53.172 --> 00:05:54.533
"Nobody really knows.

00:05:54.533 --> 00:05:57.076
"I know 'maybe' is a
very slim reed to hang

00:05:57.076 --> 00:05:59.996
"your whole life on, but
that's the best we have."

00:05:59.996 --> 00:06:02.445
And then I started to sit back,

00:06:02.445 --> 00:06:05.566
and I actually began to enjoy myself.

00:06:05.566 --> 00:06:09.296
♫ Oh, Freedonia, oh don't you cry for me

00:06:09.296 --> 00:06:13.010
- And I give it my all,
I practice every day,

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:16.709
and I try and stay authentic
and uncompromising.

00:06:16.709 --> 00:06:20.030
We don't play any crowd pleasing stuff.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:22.197
We don't get out there and

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:26.440
play "When the Saints Go Marching In."

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:30.523
We play blues, hymns,
ragtime, whore house music,

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:34.097
stuff that was authentically New Orleans,

00:06:34.097 --> 00:06:38.747
and we play it in the New
Orleans style as best we can.

00:06:38.747 --> 00:06:42.398
And so I don't feel that I'm a sell-out.

00:06:42.398 --> 00:06:44.609
I'm doing the best I can.

00:06:44.609 --> 00:06:47.493
The trick is, and it
sounds facetious sometimes

00:06:47.493 --> 00:06:50.493
but it's not, you hire great people,

00:06:51.737 --> 00:06:53.684
great actors and actresses.

00:06:53.684 --> 00:06:56.365
And then you get out of their way.

00:06:56.365 --> 00:07:01.356
You don't play the director,
you don't hover over them.

00:07:01.356 --> 00:07:03.580
You let them change your lines,

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:06.791
you let them make up their own
contributions and do things,

00:07:06.791 --> 00:07:10.499
because the people that I've worked with

00:07:10.499 --> 00:07:13.209
have had careers, and
are famous, and are great

00:07:13.209 --> 00:07:16.697
before they met me, and
they're great in my movies,

00:07:16.697 --> 00:07:18.851
and they're great after they leave me.

00:07:18.851 --> 00:07:21.452
So nothing special is happening.

00:07:21.452 --> 00:07:24.285
If you just hire wonderful people,

00:07:25.605 --> 00:07:27.340
that's all you have to do, and just

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:30.340
don't mess them up, don't ruin them.

00:07:31.363 --> 00:07:33.807
You know, it's really a very simple thing.

00:07:33.807 --> 00:07:36.262
Then you get credit for directing them,

00:07:36.262 --> 00:07:39.525
and everyone says, "Well,
you did such a great job."

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:43.701
But it isn't, you're just not
screwing them up, that's all.

00:07:43.701 --> 00:07:46.861
Successful, for me, in my
terms, are, when I'm home

00:07:46.861 --> 00:07:49.572
lying on my bed and I'm writing something,

00:07:49.572 --> 00:07:52.637
I have these incredible ideas
that I think I'm going to write

00:07:52.637 --> 00:07:54.797
Citizen Kane every time out of the box,

00:07:54.797 --> 00:07:56.315
and it's going to be great.

00:07:56.315 --> 00:07:59.928
And then I make the film,
and I'm so humiliated

00:07:59.928 --> 00:08:02.577
by what I see after, what I
think, "Where did I go wrong?

00:08:02.577 --> 00:08:05.776
"Was it the editing, was it my writing?

00:08:05.776 --> 00:08:08.018
"Was I lax in the directing?"

00:08:08.018 --> 00:08:11.146
So, when I finally get a film

00:08:11.146 --> 00:08:14.670
and I think, "Hey, this
film is pretty close

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:16.725
"to what I was writing at home,"

00:08:16.725 --> 00:08:18.749
I think, "This is a successful film."

00:08:18.749 --> 00:08:20.527
Husbands and Wives was a film like that.

00:08:20.527 --> 00:08:22.193
I thought that I brought that,

00:08:22.193 --> 00:08:25.233
that I had an idea and I brought it off.

00:08:25.233 --> 00:08:29.960
I could probably rob banks
or burglarize jewelry stores

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:31.742
and be very clever about it.

00:08:31.742 --> 00:08:34.173
I'm imaginative, I've
got a sense of humor,

00:08:34.173 --> 00:08:36.614
I've got an original approach.

00:08:36.614 --> 00:08:39.248
Maybe I could, this could be
an interesting thing for me.

00:08:39.248 --> 00:08:41.581
And I thought about it for
a while, and talked about

00:08:41.581 --> 00:08:45.798
certain con-man schemes
with my friends, but

00:08:45.798 --> 00:08:48.298
by that time, some of my jokes

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:53.687
were already being accepted
by radio and television.

00:08:54.595 --> 00:08:57.080
And I saw the light at
the end of the tunnel,

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:00.993
and I thought, "Hey, I
could probably make a living

00:09:00.993 --> 00:09:04.660
"being a comic, a writer
of comic material."

00:09:06.357 --> 00:09:10.707
And I succeeded at that
very rapidly, fortunately.

00:09:10.707 --> 00:09:14.080
As soon as I started doing
it, I started succeeding,

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:16.970
and I started getting paid for it.

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:19.970
So it saved me from a life of crime.

00:09:20.965 --> 00:09:21.965
- I do think

00:09:23.213 --> 00:09:25.996
in everybody's life there

00:09:25.996 --> 00:09:30.097
are times when there are
turning points in your life

00:09:30.097 --> 00:09:32.293
when you suddenly ...

00:09:32.293 --> 00:09:34.262
realize that ...

00:09:34.262 --> 00:09:36.179
something momentous ...

00:09:37.247 --> 00:09:41.225
can possibly happen if you make a choice.

00:09:41.225 --> 00:09:44.558
And if you make the right choice, great.

00:09:46.394 --> 00:09:50.561
In this case, the choice that
Joaquin makes is irrational.

00:09:51.464 --> 00:09:53.721
But it's not so ...

00:09:53.721 --> 00:09:55.471
irrational, given ...

00:09:56.536 --> 00:09:59.246
the comparison with the choices that

00:09:59.246 --> 00:10:01.329
we all make in our lives.

00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:05.470
If you look at ...

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:13.498
People need something to--
(phone ringing)

00:10:13.498 --> 00:10:15.267
I hear bells.

00:10:15.267 --> 00:10:19.407
People need something to
believe in in their lives.

00:10:19.407 --> 00:10:21.932
They have to choose whether their ...

00:10:21.932 --> 00:10:24.167
You choose whether life is going to be

00:10:24.167 --> 00:10:26.988
meaningful for you or meaningless.

00:10:26.988 --> 00:10:31.347
And people will choose
religions in their lives,

00:10:31.347 --> 00:10:35.667
for example, and they'll
make an irrational choice.

00:10:35.667 --> 00:10:39.276
They'll think that if they
live a very good life,

00:10:39.276 --> 00:10:43.486
they will die and go to
heaven and live in heaven

00:10:43.486 --> 00:10:45.736
for the rest of their life.

00:10:46.893 --> 00:10:49.810
That's no less crazy a thought than

00:10:50.968 --> 00:10:54.214
Joaquin thinking that
if he commits this act

00:10:54.214 --> 00:10:58.364
his life will suddenly
turn around for the better.

00:10:58.364 --> 00:11:00.572
As long as there's something

00:11:00.572 --> 00:11:03.405
dedicated that you can believe in,

00:11:04.351 --> 00:11:07.227
you make a choice and you go with it.

00:11:07.227 --> 00:11:09.810
At that point in my life I was,

00:11:12.321 --> 00:11:15.518
for whatever ridiculous reason,

00:11:15.518 --> 00:11:19.435
very taken with the films
of Chaplin and Keaton

00:11:20.375 --> 00:11:22.809
in relation to myself.

00:11:22.809 --> 00:11:24.351
Not ridiculous that I'd
be taken with their films,

00:11:24.351 --> 00:11:27.094
they're great, but I was

00:11:27.094 --> 00:11:29.177
trying to learn from them

00:11:30.066 --> 00:11:31.822
to see what I could ...

00:11:31.822 --> 00:11:35.239
And I had the idea that they were working

00:11:36.518 --> 00:11:39.404
in a silent era, and that was easier.

00:11:39.404 --> 00:11:42.342
Most people think that
silent films were harder,

00:11:42.342 --> 00:11:44.728
silent comedies were harder
'cause you didn't have

00:11:44.728 --> 00:11:47.745
a voice, you couldn't speak,
but silent films were easier.

00:11:47.745 --> 00:11:50.867
When you added the voice, it
became much more complicated,

00:11:50.867 --> 00:11:53.926
and it's really the difference
between checkers and chess.

00:11:53.926 --> 00:11:57.259
(march-like jazz music)

00:12:00.607 --> 00:12:03.607
(clarinet sqealing)

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:27.181
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:27.181 --> 00:13:30.124
I made this video because
Heghine Melkonyan asked me to.

00:13:30.124 --> 00:13:31.192
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:13:31.192 --> 00:13:32.702
that you want me to profile next,

00:13:32.702 --> 00:13:34.229
check out the link in the description,

00:13:34.229 --> 00:13:36.252
and you can go and cast your vote.

00:13:36.252 --> 00:13:37.410
I'd also love to know which clip

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:38.820
resonated the most with you.

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:40.718
What lesson are you going
to take from this video

00:13:40.718 --> 00:13:42.792
and immediately apply to your life

00:13:42.792 --> 00:13:44.203
or to your business somehow?

00:13:44.203 --> 00:13:45.606
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:13:45.606 --> 00:13:47.853
I'm really curious to find
out what you have to say.

00:13:47.853 --> 00:13:50.211
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Simon Stanley.

00:13:50.211 --> 00:13:51.737
Simon, thank you so much for picking up

00:13:51.737 --> 00:13:53.601
a copy of my book, Your One Word,

00:13:53.601 --> 00:13:56.132
and making that awesome
YouTube video on it as well.

00:13:56.132 --> 00:13:57.517
I really appreciate the support,

00:13:57.517 --> 00:13:59.557
and I'm so glad that you enjoyed my book.

00:13:59.557 --> 00:14:02.366
- In this video I'm doing a video review

00:14:02.366 --> 00:14:05.208
of Your One Word, by Evan Carmichael.

00:14:05.208 --> 00:14:06.790
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:08.519
I believe in you, I hope you continue

00:14:08.519 --> 00:14:12.281
to believe in yourself and
whatever your one word is.

00:14:12.281 --> 00:14:14.698
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:14:20.794 --> 00:14:22.195
- In all the years you've
been making movies,

00:14:22.195 --> 00:14:23.844
can you tell us what you've learned?

00:14:23.844 --> 00:14:25.782
And if you had a chance to
remake any of your movies,

00:14:25.782 --> 00:14:28.007
or if you ever wanted to
remake any of your movies,

00:14:28.007 --> 00:14:29.955
which ones would you do?

00:14:29.955 --> 00:14:34.347
- Well, you do learn
stuff, but very little.

00:14:34.347 --> 00:14:38.725
You learn stuff after the
first two or three movies,

00:14:38.725 --> 00:14:41.696
you've learned all you're
really going to learn.

00:14:41.696 --> 00:14:46.614
Most of the stuff can't be
learned, it's instinctive.

00:14:46.614 --> 00:14:49.228
There are a few technical
things that you learn

00:14:49.228 --> 00:14:53.705
about editing and adding music,
and little technical things,

00:14:53.705 --> 00:14:57.466
but you learn them very
quickly, and the rest

00:14:57.466 --> 00:15:00.593
you either rise or fail on the basis

00:15:00.593 --> 00:15:03.757
of your own talent or your own instinct.

00:15:03.757 --> 00:15:07.648
It fails you sometimes, and
sometimes it works for you.

00:15:07.648 --> 00:15:10.898
I would shoot them all over if I could.

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:16.158
Charlie Chaplin had the luxury

00:15:16.158 --> 00:15:19.173
of shooting a whole film all the time,

00:15:19.173 --> 00:15:21.282
and he could look at it and study it,

00:15:21.282 --> 00:15:24.221
then shoot it again, shoot
it again if he wanted to.

00:15:24.221 --> 00:15:26.724
Films were not that costly at that time.

00:15:26.724 --> 00:15:28.057
They had the ...

00:15:29.129 --> 00:15:32.118
the actors, the money, it was very simple.

00:15:32.118 --> 00:15:35.285
I can't do that, so when I make a film

00:15:36.297 --> 00:15:40.246
I never ever look at it
again once I put it out,

00:15:40.246 --> 00:15:44.947
because if you look at it
again, you can always see

00:15:44.947 --> 00:15:48.236
what you did wrong and
how you can improve it

00:15:48.236 --> 00:15:51.448
and why it's as terrible as it is.

00:15:51.448 --> 00:15:53.844
So I would shoot them all again.

00:15:53.844 --> 00:15:58.011
I could very happily take any
movie I have and improve it

00:15:59.106 --> 00:16:03.146
if I could get the cast back
and the circumstances exact,

00:16:03.146 --> 00:16:06.206
roll back time, get the money.

00:16:06.206 --> 00:16:08.763
I could improve them all after seeing them

00:16:08.763 --> 00:16:10.846
start to finish up there.

